Well, yes because "together with" can mean multiple things in terms of a sequence, including (after password authentication) a biometric check first and only then "something you have", whereas the intent is for biometrics to be secondary to a possession factor.

I can't immediately think of a convincing reason why such misinterpretations of "together with" can lead to bad things, but in my experience the lack of clarity alone is often the cause of poor decisions.

@randomstuff Yes, you are right on that - you can be compliant with both as is. However, ASVS explicitly allows something forbidden by NIST, even though SP 800-63B is the primary source for the V6 rules in ASVS. To my point, another example of how a single word can result in completely opposite interpretations. :)
